      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended on this curve, y equals sec x, which is one over cos x. At x equals zero it sits at one, at the bottom of a U. Picture a tower one unit tall in the sun. When the sun is x radians from straight overhead, the shadow is tan x long, and the sunbeam from the roof's edge to the shadow's tip is sec x long. With the sun straight overhead, that beam is shortest, just one. Here's a tempting guess for the derivative of sec x. Cos x has derivative minus sine x, so maybe one over cos x has derivative one over minus sine x. Just flip it. At x equals zero, that's one over zero, which doesn't even exist. But the tangent there is plainly flat. So flipping the derivative fails. How do sec, cosec and cot really change when x changes?"
    },
