      "pronunciationText": "Here's a pattern that halves what you memorise, now you know where the results come from. Pair each function with its co-partner: sine and cos, tan and cot, sec and cosec. Swap every name in a derivative for its partner, and add a minus. Sine to cos becomes cos to minus sine. Tan to sec squared becomes cot to minus cosec squared. Sec to sec tan becomes cosec to minus cosec cot. It works because each co-function is its partner of pi over two minus x, and that inside has derivative minus one. On the tower, measure the sun from the horizon instead. That angle is pi over two minus x, so the same shadow is cot of it, and the beam is cosec of it. As one angle grows, the other shrinks just as fast. That's why the co-functions carry the minus."
    },
